Aerie-Purpose: Hide an extension's cookie allow or session-only rule that could apply to a third-party context while third-party cookies are blocked, so a rule set while they were allowed stops winning over the block, and brings it back if the user allows them again.
Aerie-Upstream-Area: extensions/browser/api/content_settings/content_settings_custom_extension_provider.cc and .h; chrome/browser/content_settings/host_content_settings_map_factory.cc and cookie_settings_factory.cc
Aerie-Kind: hook

Issue #166, found by a Claude Security scan. Patch 0016 refuses a broad cookie ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY
exception from chrome.contentSettings.cookies.set only while third-party cookies are blocked at that moment.
A rule that an extension set while the user allowed third-party cookies stayed in the store, and an explicit
allow wins over the third-party block (kAllowByExplicitSetting), so it let third-party cookies through again
after the user switched back to blocking.

This patch filters on read, so nothing is dropped. While third-party cookies are blocked, the rule iterator
and GetRule of CustomExtensionProvider (the content settings provider for extension rules) leave out a
cookie ALLOW or SESSION_ONLY rule that is not first-party only (the primary pattern equals the secondary
pattern and names one host, no domain wildcard). That test is
CustomExtensionProvider::IsCookieAllowForThirdPartyContext, and patch 0016 now calls it for the write
check, so the two cannot drift. A first-party allow, every blocking rule and every rule of another type are
read as before, and rules of other providers (the user's) are never touched. The rule stays in the
extension store: if the user allows third-party cookies again it takes effect again. A hidden SESSION_ONLY
rule also loses its session-only effect on first-party cookies of that pattern while they are blocked.

"Third-party cookies are blocked" is not recomputed here. The provider asks the real
content_settings::CookieSettings of the same profile (ShouldBlockThirdPartyCookies(), the call patch 0016
makes), so the cookie controls mode, incognito, forced blocking and modifiers all count as they do for
cookie decisions. The map and its providers are built before CookieSettings (which depends on the map), so
a small ref-counted holder, ThirdPartyCookieBlocking, is kept on the Profile: the map factory gives it to
the provider, CookieSettingsFactory binds the CookieSettings to it, and it is a CookieSettings::Observer, so
a change of the blocking tells the provider's observers. It is safe on any thread (rules are read on the
IO and network threads). Until it is bound, and after shutdown, it answers "blocked", so the filter fails
closed. The provider takes the holder as a required constructor argument; the map factory is its only
construction in the tree (the unit tests that touch the extension provider slot use MockProvider). The
off-the-record profile has its own map, provider, holder and CookieSettings, so each answers for its own
profile and the incognito argument of the iterator is not used for the decision.

